   * Security and permissions: Access to audit data is restricted to
   * administrative actors. This endpoint requires the "admin" actor and the
   * provider implementation MUST enforce authorization checks that ensure only
   * authorized admin users may query sensitive fields (for example, details,
   * user/session identifiers). Query results SHOULD redact or withhold
   * sensitive data from list views when the calling admin lacks explicit
   * forensic privileges; the access gating must be implemented server-side and
   * documented in the security policy.
   *
   * Validation rules and business logic: Inputs are validated for types and
   * formats (UUID formats for actor/target ids, canonical event_type/token
   * values when applicable, and ISO-8601 date-time values for range filters).
   * Pagination parameters are bounded to safe limits by the implementation.
   * Text search terms MUST be sanitized to avoid injection in raw queries. If
   * filtering by deletion state, the implementation can reference the
   * deleted_at column to include/exclude records that have a deletion
   * timestamp.
   *
   * Related operations: Typical workflows include retrieving a set of audit
   * events with this endpoint and then retrieving a single event via GET
   * /todoApp/admin/auditLogs/{auditLogId} for full detail. For moderation
   * workflows, admins may use the audit results alongside admin action
   * endpoints to correlate actions and evidence.
   *
   * Expected behavior and error handling: Returns a paginated summary (status
   * 200) when filters are valid. Returns 400 for invalid query parameters
   * (malformed UUID, invalid date-time, invalid enum), 401/403 for unauthorized
   * callers, and 500 for unexpected server errors. The response contains
   * summary fields optimized for list display; callers should use the
   * single-item GET to obtain the full details payload when permitted.
   *
   * @param connection
